<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<!DOCTYPE properties SYSTEM 'http://java.sun.com/dtd/properties.dtd'>
<!--
  Traccar position forwarding — push each fix to the hut app as it arrives.

  WHY: the hut app used to poll Traccar every few seconds, so a fix could sit
  for up to a poll interval before the app saw it. That does not change a
  recorded finish *time* (crossings are interpolated between fix timestamps),
  but it delays the things the delay is visible in: the automatic horn, and the
  positions competitors are shown. It matters more the faster the trackers
  report — at one fix a second near the line, a 5 s poll is the bottleneck.

  This is NOT "forwarder-only" mode. Traccar keeps its normal database, so it
  keeps the device registry (needed to send commands to a tracker, e.g. raising
  its reporting rate near the line), the history the app back-fills from after
  an outage, and its web UI. Forwarding works alongside all of that.

  HOW TO APPLY on the relay:
    1. In the hut app: Settings -> GPS tracking -> "Push ingest token". Generate a
       long random string and save it.
    2. Merge the entries below into /opt/traccar/conf/traccar.xml, replacing
       PUT_THE_SAME_TOKEN_HERE with that token.
    3. systemctl restart traccar
    4. Watch the app's Settings -> GPS tracking status box. Its "Push:" line
       reads "working - N fixes received, last just now" once fixes are
       arriving. If it still says "configured, but no fix has arrived this way
       yet", the token here does not match the one in Settings -- which fails
       silently, because the app just carries on polling.
    5. Once push is working you can raise the app's poll interval (Settings ->
       GPS tracking -> Poll interval) to ~30 s. It stays as the safety net: it
       still catches up after an outage and covers a forwarder that has stalled.

  If the app is unreachable, Traccar retries and queues up to forward.retry.limit
  positions; anything beyond that the app back-fills from Traccar's history on
  its next poll.
-->
<properties>

    <!-- JSON forwarder: POSTs {"position": {...}, "device": {...}} per fix. -->
    <entry key='forward.enable'>true</entry>
    <entry key='forward.type'>json</entry>
    <entry key='forward.url'>https://pro.pwllhelisailingclub.org/api/track/ingest</entry>

    <!-- Shared secret. Must match Settings -> GPS tracking -> Push ingest token.
         The app rejects anything without it (401), and stays closed entirely
         while no token is configured (503). -->
    <entry key='forward.header'>X-RO-Track-Token: PUT_THE_SAME_TOKEN_HERE</entry>

    <!-- Ride out a brief hut outage rather than dropping fixes on the floor. -->
    <entry key='forward.retry.enable'>true</entry>
    <entry key='forward.retry.delay'>1000</entry>
    <entry key='forward.retry.count'>10</entry>
    <entry key='forward.retry.limit'>100</entry>

    <!--
      OPTIONAL — automatic device enrolment.

      With this on, a tracker that connects is created in Traccar automatically,
      and it then shows up under "Trackers seen on the network" on the app's
      Trackers page, where a race officer picks the boat and presses Add — no
      15-digit IMEI to type. Note the trade-off: the Queclink port is open to the
      internet, so anyone who connects to it can cause a device row to be
      created. Nothing is tracked until a race officer assigns that device to a
      boat, but the list will collect noise; delete strays in Traccar.
    -->
    <!-- <entry key='database.registerUnknown'>true</entry> -->

</properties>
